 :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cc9f8a6-afe7-5e92-8136-dfa76f9850bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380932} :answer-field/id #uuid "817a224e-d1d2-5a53-a896-499e48c5e570" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf13071c-d3b1-5868-a0cc-3bf7e1adbfaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-333589" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $v=[3,\\text{ }3]^{T}$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }3]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{{field-1}}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}{{field-2}}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ 3t+3\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{4t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/assets/6ec9eac46644188b8b41a1a7ea28ca0542ee7e5c69d713028481904e90dd557a.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ 3t+3\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y=3(t+1){e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.20$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $0.20$ | $3$ | {{field-8}} |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/assets/b0be26a1544bbb3117afab15d004bafee70d831fffab4b1f6921870871ac4768.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/assets/64e685f4ea06a8f497ddfbe50a092a0362b8c92a9d2f78f9da2a8164113a785a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =4,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ 3t+3\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/assets/6ec9eac46644188b8b41a1a7ea28ca0542ee7e5c69d713028481904e90dd557a.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ 3t+3\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3t{e}^{4t} \\\\ y=3(t+1){e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.20$ | $0$ | $0.45$ |\n| $y$ | $0.20$ | $3$ | $4.92$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/assets/b0be26a1544bbb3117afab15d004bafee70d831fffab4b1f6921870871ac4768.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/assets/64e685f4ea06a8f497ddfbe50a092a0362b8c92a9d2f78f9da2a8164113a785a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2c335ee-467a-5114-a7d9-4c2692562d9e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3df03b28-58fd-5bda-88e5-bf2dce7eba1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380937} {:answer/id #uuid "ad164a18-b7fd-51bb-9cf6-859f915c1257" :